+### 4.5 Existing-data gate battery (post-hoc deconfounding — Gates 1, 1b, 2, 3, trigger #7)
+
+After the primary analysis, five existing-data gates — specified by the GPT Pro forward-analysis (`reports/reviews/2026-06-16_gptpro_v0_3_forward_analysis.md`) and mapping onto predeclared failure-triggers #7/#8 — stress-tested whether the three personalization-relevant headlines (specificity, matching, structure) reflect target-specific adaptation or shared formatting / a global trait-pole preference. These are **post-hoc, exploratory** diagnostics (not predeclared contrasts); surface features are post-treatment mediators, so they are conservative adversarial controls, not clean causal estimates. Full detail and scripts: `reports/analysis/gate_battery_findings.md`, `drivers/analysis/gate{1,1b,2,3}_*.py`, `trigger7_judge_divergence.py`.
+
+| Gate | Question | Result | Effect on headline |
+|------|----------|--------|--------------------|
+| **1 + 1b** (trigger #8) | Do headlines survive surface / stylometric control? | Specificity (C3/C4 > C_GENERIC) does **NOT** survive (surface-adjusted CIs include 0.5 under both a 3-feature and a 9-feature index); matching (C3/C4 > C4_WRONG) and structure (> C0) **do** | **Specificity → a surface effect** |
+| **2** | Is matching real target-fit, or a global trait-pole preference? (reciprocal sign-flip) | **3 of 4** reciprocal pairs are global-pole — one persona's profile wins regardless of which persona is the target | **Matching is mostly global-pole, not target-fit** |
+| **3** | Do the profiles produce target-distinct text? (blind, non-study coder) | **Yes** — 93% blind recovery of the conditioning persona [0.872, 0.963]; all 3 global-pole pairs sign-flip at the *text* level (both directions ≥0.75) | **Generation works; the matching failure is at evaluation** |
+| **#7** | Is "structure" judge-unanimous? | **No** — fires on 5/11 covered contrasts, all contract-structure; opus-judge rewards contracts (68–91%), gpt-judge at/below chance | **Structure floor is largely an opus-as-judge effect** |
+
+**Synthesis.** The three personalization-relevant claims are each undercut by a different gate, and jointly they paint a sharper — and partly constructive — picture than any single contrast. Persona conditioning *does* produce target-distinct outputs (**Gate 3**: 93% blind-recoverable; the profiles steer text bidirectionally even on the pairs where the study judge shows a global-pole), but the *judges* reward a global trait-pole and instruction-salient contract scaffolding, not fit-to-target. Specifically: (a) the specificity increment over a generic contract is a **surface effect** (Gates 1/1b); (b) the structure floor is **concentrated in opus-as-judge** (#7), so "any contract beats baseline" is not judge-unanimous (the aggregate 65.6% averages opus-judge ~90% with gpt-judge ~50%); (c) the matching increment is the judges' **global trait-pole preference coinciding with the correct profile** (Gate 2), and Gate 3 localizes this to the **evaluation** stage — in 25% of pairs the author wrote correctly target-aligned text but the judge still preferred the *opposite*-conditioned output. The crucial design asymmetry: the C4-vs-C4_WRONG contrast asked judges for *unconditional* quality with no persona reference, so it measured "is P-conditioned text unconditionally better than Q-conditioned text" (which tracks a global pole), **not** "is P-conditioned text better *for P*." The latter needs target-conditioned judging (v0.4). **Caveat**: Gate 3's coder is a single non-study model whose stated bases often cite surface contract-echo, so "target-distinct" is partly surface contract-*following* — which, if anything, strengthens the eval-bottleneck reading (the distinctness is detectable yet unrewarded). Net, this is the honest v0.3 headline: **the bottleneck is evaluation, not generation.**
