 ## 11. Closing
 
-v0.3 set out to take the v0.2 "behavioral contracts beat trait conditioning" result apart and ask *which part does the work*. The answer is layered: a well-formed behavioral contract is the largest single factor — present even when generic, even when mismatched — and on top of that floor, using the *correct* profile and anchoring it to a *recognizable public figure* each add a reliable but smaller (and, for the public anchor, confound-shadowed) increment. Within the contract itself, contract-first ordering is the only individually-detectable step, with the single-path caveats of §4.3. The honest counterweight runs through the whole story: roughly half the over-baseline advantage is matching-independent, the cross-provider scope leaves a shared-formatting-preference null largely unconstrained, and the causal "mechanism" reading is gated on an analysis not yet run. So the safest summary is descriptive — this is a map of how three model families, judging blind and position-controlled, respond to seven ways of conditioning a synthetic persona, not yet a claim that any of it reflects personalization efficacy or transfers to real users. The map is now detailed enough to design the validation (v0.4) that would tell us which.
+v0.3 set out to take the v0.2 "behavioral contracts beat trait conditioning" result apart and ask *which part does the work*. The forced-choice surface showed a layered structure — a well-formed contract as the largest factor, with correct-profile and public-anchor increments on top. **The existing-data gate battery (§4.5) then relocated the explanation.** Two of the personalization-relevant layers dissolve under scrutiny: the specificity increment is a surface effect (it does not survive length/stylometry control), and the structure floor is not judge-unanimous (it is largely an opus-as-judge preference; gpt-as-judge is at chance on contract-vs-baseline). The matching layer is real in forced choice but is mostly a *global trait-pole* preference rather than target-fit. The one finding that *survives* the skeptical gauntlet is the most constructive: blind, non-study coding shows the conditioning genuinely **produces target-distinct text** (93% recoverable, sign-flipping in both directions on the very pairs where the study judge shows a global-pole). So the personalization bottleneck v0.3 actually exposes is **evaluation, not generation**: the authors adapt the text to the persona; the judges — asked for *unconditional* quality with no target in view — reward a global pole and instruction-salient scaffolding instead. The safest summary is no longer just descriptive: persona conditioning works at the generation stage, and the load-bearing problem to solve next is the *judge*. That reframes v0.4 — **target-conditioned ("better *for* persona P") judging, per-judge calibration, and trait-pole-balanced persona sampling become the priority over better profiles** — and it sharpens, rather than abandons, the construct-validity question: does target-fit text actually serve the target? Only judging that asks that question can say.
 
